Two branches each add a step, and git merges ledger.sql as it merges code:
both appended lines meet at the end of the file, a conflict the person
settles by putting one after the other. Production, which ran neither,
runs both in that order; the steps of one branch never see the other's
columns, and need not.

1. Release 2 renames `title` to `name`.
2. Branch A adds `slug`, filled from the name; branch B adds `note`,
   filled with a default text. Merged: A's steps, then B's.

== before
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;
INSERT INTO dish VALUES (1, 'Pea Soup'), (2, 'Rye Bread');

== after
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;

== ledger
ALTER TABLE dish RENAME COLUMN title TO name;

== after
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, slug TEXT NOT NULL, note TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;

== ledger
ALTER TABLE dish RENAME COLUMN title TO name;
-- Branch A: slugs.
ALTER TABLE dish ADD COLUMN slug TEXT;
UPDATE dish SET slug = lower(replace(name, ' ', '-'));
-- Branch B: notes.
ALTER TABLE dish ADD COLUMN note TEXT;
UPDATE dish SET note = 'none yet';

